			<!-- H2 Section 2 -->
			<section style="margin-bottom: 3.5rem;">
				<h2 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.85rem; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1rem;">
					2. Place Cards vs. Escort Cards: Key Etiquette & Differences
				</h2>
				<p>
					Couples frequently confuse <strong>wedding place cards</strong> with <strong>wedding escort cards</strong>, yet they fulfill two completely distinct logistical functions during a wedding reception. Misunderstanding this difference often results in guests wandering the ballroom aimlessly or banquet servers serving beef to vegetarian attendees.
				</p>
				
				<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 1.5rem; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
					<div style="background: #f8fafc; border: 2px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.5rem;">
						<strong style="color: #0f172a; font-size: 1.15rem; display: block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🎴 Escort Cards (At the Entrance Table)</strong>
						<p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #475569; line-height: 1.7; margin: 0;">
							Escort cards are displayed alphabetically by last name at the cocktail hour or ballroom entrance. Each card informs the guest which <strong>table number</strong> they have been assigned to (e.g., <em>"Marcus Vance — Table 4"</em>). The guest picks up their card and walks to their assigned table.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div style="background: #f0fdf4; border: 2px solid #a7f3d0; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.5rem;">
						<strong style="color: #166534; font-size: 1.15rem; display: block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🏷️ Place Cards (At the Specific Dinner Chair)</strong>
						<p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #14532d; line-height: 1.7; margin: 0;">
							Place cards reside directly on the dinner napkin or charger plate at the specific chair where the guest must sit. They establish assigned seating per chair (rather than just open seating at the table) and display the guest's <strong>pre-selected meal choice</strong> to catering staff.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.35rem; color: var(--accent-red); margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;">
					Plus-One and Couple Place Card Wording Etiquette
				</h3>
				<p>
					Always write the full proper name of every adult guest whenever possible. Avoid writing <em>"John Smith and Guest"</em> on a place card. Reach out to your attendee before printing to obtain their companion's first and last name:
				</p>
				<ul style="line-height: 1.8; margin-left: 1.5rem;">
					<li><strong>Married Couples:</strong> If seated at adjacent chairs, each should still receive their own individual place card with their specific meal selection (e.g., <em>"Mr. David Miller"</em> and <em>"Mrs. Sarah Miller"</em>).</li>
					<li><strong>Couples with Different Last Names:</strong> List each full name on their respective card (e.g., <em>"Dr. Rachel Hayes"</em> and <em>"Mr. Thomas Bennett"</em>).</li>
					<li><strong>Children:</strong> Include the child's first name with a designated kids' meal icon (e.g. 🍗 Chicken Tenders) to ensure kitchen staff deliver kid-friendly portions.</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<!-- H2 Section 3 -->
			<section style="margin-bottom: 3.5rem;">
				<h2 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.85rem; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1rem;">
					3. Caterer Meal Indicators: How to Prevent Banquet Service Delays
				</h2>
				<p>
					For multi-choice plated catering (such as choice of beef, chicken, fish, or vegetarian), catering captains require a visible indicator on each place card. Without meal markers, waitstaff must interrupt speeches to ask 150 guests what entrée they selected 4 months ago on their RSVP card, causing courses to get cold and reception timelines to run 30–45 minutes late.
				</p>

				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.35rem; color: var(--accent-blue); margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;">
					Universal Dietary Symbols & Icon Placement Standards
				</h3>
				<p>
					The cleanest industry practice is placing a small, discrete 14–18px meal symbol in the <strong>bottom-right corner</strong> or <strong>directly below the guest name</strong>:
				</p>
				<div style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1.5px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 6px; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1rem 0;">
					<ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; line-height: 1.8;">
						<li><strong>🥩 Beef / Steak:</strong> A cow silhouette or meat symbol.</li>
						<li><strong>🍗 Chicken / Poultry:</strong> A bird or feather icon.</li>
						<li><strong>🐟 Fish / Seafood:</strong> A fish motif.</li>
						<li><strong>🥦 Vegetarian:</strong> A leaf, sprig, or vegetable indicator.</li>
						<li><strong>🌱 Vegan:</strong> A double-sprout symbol.</li>
						<li><strong>🌾 Gluten-Free (GF):</strong> A wheat-cross icon or clear bold text.</li>
						<li><strong>🧒 Kids Meal:</strong> A smile or small icon for child-friendly portions.</li>
						<li><strong>⚪ None / Blank:</strong> For single-course banquets or open buffet service.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- H2 Section 4 -->
			<section style="margin-bottom: 3.5rem;">
				<h2 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.85rem; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1rem;">
					4. Cardstock Weight, Sizing Standards & DIY Home Printing Guide
				</h2>
				<p>
					The single biggest mistake couples make when printing place cards at home is using standard 20lb copy paper or flimsy 65lb cardstock. Lightweight paper wilts under reception ballroom humidity, blows away in outdoor marquee breezes, and sags on dinner plates.
				</p>

				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.35rem; color: var(--accent-blue); margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;">
					Optimal Paper Weight: 80lb vs 100lb vs 110lb Cover (GSM Breakdown)
				</h3>
				<ul style="line-height: 1.8; margin-left: 1.5rem;">
					<li><strong>80lb Cover (216 GSM):</strong> The minimum acceptable weight for flat escort cards inserted into wooden or acrylic card stands.</li>
					<li><strong>100lb to 110lb Cover (270–300 GSM):</strong> The gold standard for <strong>Folded Tent Cards</strong>. This heavyweight cardstock has the structural integrity to stand upright like a mini tent throughout a 5-hour dinner service.</li>
					<li><strong>Linen & Cotton Texture:</strong> Look for 100lb linen-weave or 100% cotton rag cardstock for a luxurious, tactile finish that rivals professional letterpress studios.</li>
				</ul>

				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.35rem; color: var(--accent-red); margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;">
					How to Score & Fold Tent Cards Without Paper Fiber Cracking
				</h3>
				<p>
					When folding heavy 100lb+ cardstock, folding directly by hand will crack the paper fibers and create an uneven, frayed spine. Follow this professional scoring technique:
				</p>
				<ol style="line-height: 1.8; margin-left: 1.5rem;">
					<li>Align a metal ruler along the dashed center fold line generated on your printed PDF sheet.</li>
					<li>Run a <strong>bone folder</strong> (or the dull backside of a butter knife) along the ruler edge applying moderate pressure to indent the paper fibers.</li>
					<li>Fold the card toward the indentation for a razor-sharp, crease-free edge that stands upright effortlessly.</li>
				</ol>
			</section>

